---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import StepNav from "../components/StepNav.astro";

// Rule of Thirds — hover/tap swaps the photo for a grid overlay
import ROT1 from "../assets/_DSC9072.jpg";
import ROT2 from "../assets/_DSC9072ROT.jpg";
// Centered Composition & Symmetry — hover/tap swaps for a grid overlay
import ROT3 from "../assets/_DSC6779.jpg";
import ROT4 from "../assets/_DSC6779ROT.jpg";
// Foreground Interest and Depth
import Foreground1 from "../assets/20170423-_DSC8710-Edit.jpg";
import Foreground2 from "../assets/20190504_10086DSC_5956.jpg";
// Frame within the Frame
import FrameinFrame from "../assets/20160417-_DSC4419.jpg";
// Leading Lines
import LeadingLines from "../assets/20190322_9005DSC_4880_1.jpg";
// Fill the Frame
import fillFrame1 from "../assets/_DSC8879.jpg";
import fillFrame2 from "../assets/DSC_8076.jpg";
// Isolate the Subject
import Isolate from "../assets/20170722-_DSC9588.jpg";
// Leave Negative Space
import Negativespace from "../assets/20210403_1213_DSC_9516.jpg";
// Rule of Space
import Ruleofspace from "../assets/_DSC2333.jpg";
// Change your Point of View
import Pointofview from "../assets/20190421_9784DSC_5659.jpg";
// Left to Right Rule
import Seanmotion from "../assets/seanmotion.jpg";
// Golden Ratio — hover/tap swaps the photo for a Phi grid overlay
import Goldenratio1 from "../assets/goldenratio.jpg";
import Goldenratio2 from "../assets/goldenratiooverlay.jpg";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Step 4: The Subject IS the Subject"
	description="Composition and subject — making the subject the subject of your photograph."
>
	<section class="wrapper">
		<h1 class="page-title">THE SUBJECT IS THE SUBJECT</h1>

		<article class="article">
			<p class="text">
				The subject of this subject is the subject. Images can depict
				anything and there is a way to compose them that will be pleasing
				for others too. Composition is the structure of an image. Even if
				the exposure is spot on, if the composition is off or unpleasing,
				your image will be weak. At it's most basic points, lines, and
				shapes are the elements of composition. Composition should be
				deliberate. Elements and lines should be intentionally placed in
				an image. I'm a big fan of the saying "Plan your work and work
				your plan". This means that you should know what you want to
				create an image of before you create the image. You will have an
				opportunity to think about how to compose your imaage and what you
				want the image to convey. Time of day and environmental setting
				are important factors to consider. The type of light, and
				equipment you will have available, and how long it will take you
				to set up or prepare to create the image. Deciding about all of
				the prep work will also help in deciding how and where to place
				items in your image to make a strong composition. Below is a list
				of things to consider about composing your image. They aren't hard
				and fast rules, they are guidelines and are meant to help make a
				strong composition.
			</p>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Rule of Thirds</h2>
				<button
					class="swap img-left"
					type="button"
					aria-pressed="false"
					aria-label="Rule of Thirds example — activate to overlay the grid lines on the photo"
				>
					<Image
						class="swap-base"
						src={ROT1}
						alt="An oystercatcher wading bird standing on a sandy beach with the ocean and sky behind it"
					/>
					<Image
						class="swap-overlay"
						src={ROT2}
						alt="The same beach photo with a rule-of-thirds grid overlaid, the bird placed on the right vertical line"
					/>
				</button>
				<p class="text">
					Divide the frame into 9 equal rectangles, 3 across and 3 down
					place the important element(s) of the image along one or more of
					the lines or where the lines intersect. Move your mouse cursor
					over the image and you will see the lines appear. For cell
					phones, tap on the image (double tap to see it larger) This
					image actually uses the Rule of Thirds both horizontally with
					the sand, ocean and sky and vertically placing the bird (Oyster
					Catcher) on a vertical line on the right. It also uses the Rule
					of Space and breaks the Left to Right rule.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Centered Composition and Symmetry</h2>
				<button
					class="swap img-left"
					type="button"
					aria-pressed="false"
					aria-label="Centered composition example — activate to overlay the grid lines on the photo"
				>
					<Image
						class="swap-base"
						src={ROT3}
						alt="Hopetoun House mansion in Queensferry, Scotland, mirrored in the still water in front of it"
					/>
					<Image
						class="swap-overlay"
						src={ROT4}
						alt="The Hopetoun House photo with a rule-of-thirds grid overlaid, showing its symmetry"
					/>
				</button>
				<p class="text">
					Symmetrical scenes are perfect for a centered composition.
					Scenes containing reflections are also an opportunity to use
					symmetry in the composition. Try a mix of the rule of thirds and
					symmetry to compose the image. The image of the Hopetoun House
					in Queensferry Scotland uses the Rule of Thirds well and it is
					also perfectly symmetrical. Mouse over or tap to see the grid
					lines.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">
					Foreground Interest and Depth <span class="comp-note">(left or right)</span>
				</h2>
				<Image
					class="img-right"
					src={Foreground1}
					alt="A lighthouse with rocks in the foreground adding depth and leading the eye to the subject"
				/>
				<Image
					class="img-left"
					src={Foreground2}
					alt="A waterfall with foreground rocks that add depth and lead toward the main subject"
				/>
				<p class="text">
					Good for showing and creating space for the subject in your
					image to occupy. Including foreground interest is a way of
					adding depth to the scene.In these images the rocks in the
					foreground add depth and lead the viewer to the main subject.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Frame Within the Frame</h2>
				<Image
					class="img-left"
					src={FrameinFrame}
					alt="Arches of a building forming a frame within the frame around the scene beyond"
				/>
				<p class="text">
					Including a frame within the frame is a way of portraying
					depth. Look for elements such as windows, arches or
					overhanging tree branches to frame the image with.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Leading Lines</h2>
				<Image
					class="img-left"
					src={LeadingLines}
					alt="A monument with the moon above it, its walls and lines leading attention toward the subject"
				/>
				<p class="text">
					Leading lines help focus attention on important elements. Paths,
					walls or patterns can be leading lines and they do not have to
					be straight.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Fill the Frame</h2>
				<Image
					class="img-right"
					src={fillFrame1}
					alt="A close-up of a statue filling the frame with no space around it"
				/>
				<Image
					class="img-left"
					src={fillFrame2}
					alt="An owl's face filling the frame, focusing attention on the subject with no distractions"
				/>
				<p class="text">
					Filling the frame with your subject, leaving little or no space
					around it can be effective. It helps focus the viewer completely
					on the main subject without any distractions.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Isolate the Subject</h2>
				<Image
					class="img-left"
					src={Isolate}
					alt="A bird on a branch isolated against a blurred background using a shallow depth of field"
				/>
				<p class="text">
					Using a shallow depth of field to isolate your subject will
					focus attention on the subject.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Leave Negative Space</h2>
				<Image
					class="img-left"
					src={Negativespace}
					alt="A single bird surrounded by empty sky, using negative space for a simple, minimal composition"
				/>
				<p class="text">
					Leaving a lot of empty or 'negative' space around your subject
					creates a sense of simplicity and minimalism.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Change your Point of View</h2>
				<Image
					class="img-left"
					src={Pointofview}
					alt="Baby swans (cygnets) in the water photographed from a low point of view at their level"
				/>
				<p class="text">
					Try getting high up or low down or shot from a different angle
					to compose your image. Study your subject to find what is
					pleasing and interesting. Sometimes getting on the same level
					is the best angle.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Rule of Space</h2>
				<Image
					class="img-left"
					src={Ruleofspace}
					alt="A bird flying with more space left in front of it so it can move into the frame"
				/>
				<p class="text">
					The rule of space relates to the direction the subject(s) in
					your photo are facing. Leave more space in front of the
					subject so that it can "move" into it.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Left to Right Rule</h2>
				<Image
					class="img-left"
					src={Seanmotion}
					alt="A child running from left to right while another child and a tree anchor the left side of the frame"
				/>
				<p class="text">
					There is a theory that says we read an image from left to
					right in the same way we would read a book. Here my bionic son
					is running from left to right while my daughter and the tree
					anchor the left side of the image.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="comp">
				<h2 class="comp-heading">Golden Ratio</h2>
				<button
					class="swap img-left"
					type="button"
					aria-pressed="false"
					aria-label="Golden Ratio example — activate to overlay the Phi grid and Fibonacci spiral on the photo"
				>
					<Image
						class="swap-base"
						src={Goldenratio1}
						alt="A red fox sitting in the grass"
					/>
					<Image
						class="swap-overlay"
						src={Goldenratio2}
						alt="The red fox photo overlaid with a golden-ratio Phi grid and Fibonacci spiral, the fox's eye at the tightest point of the spiral"
					/>
				</button>
				<p class="text">
					Two quantities are in the golden ratio if their ratio is the
					same as the ratio of their sum to the larger of the two
					quantities. Move your mouse cursor over the image or tap on the
					image. The frame is divided into a series of squares known as a
					"Phi Grid". Use the squares to draw a spiral called a "Fibonacci
					Spiral". It is believed that the golden spiral method of
					composition has been in existence for over 2,400 years having
					been devised in Ancient Greece.  Notice how the eyes of the
					subject fall right in the smallest box and tightest area of the
					spiral.
				</p>
			</section>

			<StepNav prev={{ href: "/step3" }} />
		</article>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
	// Accessible image-swap: each .swap is a <button>, so Enter/Space fire
	// click natively (keyboard) and tap fires it on touch. Hover is a pure-CSS
	// enhancement on top. Clicking toggles a "pinned" overlay state.
	document.querySelectorAll(".swap").forEach((btn) => {
		btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
			const active = btn.classList.toggle("is-active");
			btn.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(active));
		});
	});
</script>

<style>
	.wrapper {
		padding: 0.5em;
		max-width: 100%;
	}

	.article {
		font-size: 1em;
		margin-top: 2em;
	}

	.text {
		font-size: 1.15em;
	}

	/* Each composition guideline; overflow:auto contains the floated image. */
	.comp {
		margin-top: 20px;
		overflow: auto;
	}

	.comp-heading {
		float: left;
		width: 100%;
		margin: 0 0 0.25em;
		font-size: 1em;
		font-weight: bold;
	}
	.comp-note {
		font-weight: normal;
	}

	/* Floated images: full-ish width on phones, a quarter column on wider
	   screens (mirrors the old Img base + ImgL/ImgR float). */
	.img-left,
	.img-right {
		margin: 5px;
		max-width: 75%;
		height: auto;
	}
	.img-left {
		float: left;
	}
	.img-right {
		float: right;
	}
	@media (min-width: 650px) {
		.img-left,
		.img-right {
			max-width: 25%;
		}
	}

	/* Hover/tap image-swap control. The base image is in normal flow and sets
	   the box size; the overlay is layered on top and fades in. */
	.swap {
		position: relative;
		display: block;
		padding: 0;
		border: 0;
		background: none;
		cursor: pointer;
		line-height: 0;
	}
	.swap :global(img) {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
	}
	.swap-overlay {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		opacity: 0;
		transition: opacity 0.15s ease;
	}
	.swap:hover .swap-overlay,
	.swap:focus-visible .swap-overlay,
	.swap.is-active .swap-overlay {
		opacity: 1;
	}
	.swap:focus-visible {
		outline: 3px solid var(--link);
		outline-offset: 2px;
	}
</style>
